        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 232,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 400,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 233,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58920,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 233,
        "headingText": "<p>Most PILAs involve the proximal extremities, especially the buttock or thigh {10478659}, and less commonly the distal extremities or trunk, head and neck, intra-abdominal sites, or parenchymal sites (including testis) {10971050}. Intraosseous locations are rarely reported {10478659; 10197456; 24063649; 29423171}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-02-11T11:20:41"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 401,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 234,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58921,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 234,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There is a slight female predilection (M:F&nbsp;ratio: ~0.7:1). PILA presents as a slow-growing asymptomatic cutaneous induration, plaque, or rarely nodule, often with unremarkable overlying skin. Radiology data are scant; intraosseous tumours are lytic and destructive and may be multifocal {24063649; 29423171}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 402,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 235,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58922,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 235,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>PILA is exceedingly rare, with &lt;&nbsp;50&nbsp;reported cases {5343389; 10478659}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 403,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 236,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58923,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 236,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 404,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 237,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58924,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 237,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 411,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 244,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58925,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 244,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours are ill defined and usually involve dermis and subcutaneous tissue {10478659}. Grossly, greyish-white to pink streaks or cystic change in otherwise normal dermis and subcutis may be present, without haemorrhage or necrosis. Tumour size ranges from 1 to &gt;&nbsp;40&nbsp;cm (mean: 7&nbsp;cm) {10478659}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 405,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 238,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58926,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 238,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>PILA is an extensive, poorly delineated dermal and/or subcutaneous proliferation of lymphatic channels. A cavernous lymphangioma is often present nearby. The cavernous or slit-like lymphatic vascular channels are lined by columnar, matchstick-like, or hobnail endothelial cells. In addition, variable proliferations of intraluminal papillary tufts are noted, with hyaline cores and intermixed intraluminal lymphocytes with proteinaceous fluid. The lesional cells have eosinophilic cytoplasm and round uniform nuclei with indistinct nucleoli; they lack cytological atypia and mitoses {10478659}. Tumour cells express pan-endothelial markers, such as CD31, ERG, and less often CD34, as well as lymphatic endothelial markers, such as podoplanin, VEGFR3, and PROX1 {10478659; 22067331; 10971050}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 406,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 239,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58927,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 239,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:51:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 407,
